Description: CVE-2026-109619 - Avoid use of stale request state before RINIT
 The session upload progress handler (rfc1867) runs while the request is being
 started, before RINIT, and used to decode and re-encode the session with the
 regular unserializer. That could autoload classes, call
 unserialize_callback_func, __wakeup() or Serializable::unserialize() and so run
 user code against request-owned state that was already freed by the previous
 request on a persistent worker (use-after-free).
 .
 - The upload progress handler now decodes and re-encodes the session in an
   "incomplete classes only" mode: no class is ever looked up, every object
   is kept as __PHP_Incomplete_Class and written back unchanged (O: and C:).
   Session data carrying the reserved bookkeeping property names is rejected.
 - The handler bails out if the session could not be initialized, resets the
   session globals before use, and removes $_SESSION afterwards.
 - Globals that may be accessed during request startup (user error/exception
   handlers, objects store, EG(exception), PG(http_globals),
   PG(modules_activated), serialize/unserialize and URL rewriter state) are
   reset at request shutdown / early at request startup instead of keeping
   pointers into the freed request heap.
 .
 Backport of the CVE-2026-109619 part of php-src commit 01a40e89 to PHP 7.4.
 The delayed-OOM / fallible allocation infrastructure used for
 CVE-2026-109618 is not backported.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/php/php-src/commit/01a40e89fbfc499936e3b05888d8f00bfde886df
Bug: https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp
Author: Arnaud Le Blanc <arnaud.lb@gmail.com>
Last-Update: 2026-10-25
